[38;5;252m[1m## C behaviour deliberately reproduced rather than fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The differential suites were built specifically to pin these down:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **Sign extension in [38;5;10msiphash_bytes[0m.** Both 32-bit halves of the data word are assembled with [38;5;10mint[0m arithmetic, so a byte ≥ [38;5;10m0x80[0m at position 3 produces a negative [38;5;10mint[0m that sign-extends when converted to [38;5;10msize_t[0m, filling the upper half with ones. This is observable: for a buffer whose byte 11 is ≥ [38;5;10m0x80[0m, lengths 12–15 all hash identically. The [38;5;10mcase 4:[0m tail byte has the same flaw. Reproduced via explicit [38;5;10mas i32 as i64 as u64[0m casts.[0m[0m
- **The seed is dead.** Each [38;5;10mv0..v3[0m initialiser XORs [38;5;10mseed[0m (or [38;5;10m~seed[0m) in twice, so it cancels entirely — [38;5;10mstbds_hash_bytes[0m ignores its [38;5;10mseed[0m argument.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mtemp_key[0m is never initialised** by [38;5;10mstbds_make_hash_index[0m, and [38;5;10mstbds_hmput_key[0m skips updating it in the second (wrap-around) probe loop but not the first. Left asymmetric.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mstbds_hmdel_key[0m is broken for [38;5;10mmode == 2[0m**, passing the key field's [3maddress[23m where a [38;5;10mchar*[0m is expected. Both libraries abort at the same assert.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mstbds_arrgrowf(NULL, n, 0, 0)[0m returns [38;5;10mNULL[0m**, so callers that dereference the header crash.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10massert()[0m is live in the C (the [38;5;10mCMakeLists.txt[0m sets no build type, so no [38;5;10m-DNDEBUG[0m). I wired all ten asserts to [38;5;10m__assert_fail[0m at their original line numbers and function names, so a violation aborts with [38;5;10mSIGABRT[0m and the same message rather than continuing into undefined behaviour. The only unavoidable difference is the [38;5;10m__FILE__[0m string, which in C is the build machine's absolute path.[0m[0m
